      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In the 1880s the Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe Railroad reached the area and extended its tracks from Albuquerque toward Gallup.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.theroute-66.com/paraje.html",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "They built a station at the small town of Paraje and named it Cubero although it was almost 8 miles from the village of Cubero.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.theroute-66.com/paraje.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Parajes in New Mexico served as vital campgrounds and trade hubs along El Camino Real, where caravans rested and exchanged goods between Mexico City and Santa Fe.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/paraje",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The root verb parar traces back to the Latin parāre, originally meaning 'to prepare' or 'to arrange.'.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/paraje",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Parajes typically are spaced 10 to 15 miles apart and feature abundant water and fodder for the travelers' animals (oxen, cattle, sheep, donkeys, mules, and horses).",
